course: dictionary
lesson: 7264
title: Many meanings of pile
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the words around it tell you which one you need. Pile is a good example. «A hedgehog curled up in a pile of leaves in winter» means a heap of leaves, with things put one on top of another.
explain: Look at the clues. «Piles of homework» means a lot of homework, not a heap you can hold. «Big posts pushed into the ground to hold up a bridge» shows that pile can also mean a post. Ask yourself: what would fit in this place? Then try your meaning in the sentence to check it.
explain: Pile can also be an action, so it is a verb too. «Toad's coach-house is piled up to the roof» means things have been heaped up, not just put in one spot. In maths, sorting into piles means making equal groups, so you can work out how many are in each pile.
fact: A pile is a heap of things put one on top of another.
fact: «Piles of» or «a pile of» means a lot of something, as in «piles of homework».
fact: To pile (up) means to put things in a heap, or to grow into one. The verb forms are pile, piles, piled and piling.
fact: A pile can also be a big post pushed into the ground to hold up a building or bridge.
fact: In maths, equal piles mean equal groups, and per pile means the total divided by the number of piles.
exercise: «A hedgehog curled up in a pile of leaves in winter.» What is a pile here? | A long, thin stick | A tiny hedgehog | *A heap of leaves | A winter coat
exercise: «Piles of homework» means what? | *A lot of homework | Homework done neatly | A very small amount of homework | Only one piece of homework
exercise: In maths, equal piles means what? | Numbers that are much bigger | Things that are broken | Piles of rubbish | *Equal groups
test: Which form fits? «Yesterday Ella ___ her books into a tall stack.» | piling | *piled | pile | piles
test: In «a bridge held up by big piles», what does piles mean? | *Big posts pushed into the ground | Heaps of rubbish | A lot of money | Piles of sweets
exam: «A clever reader sorts every story into four piles.» What does pile mean here? | A pile of money | Piled up to the roof | *A group of things put together | A post holding up a bridge
